8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7cf4d71-17d1-5d39-bf9c-739bde65f8a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115510 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1061 44256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1061 44257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a07d4a92-4626-5d50-92cc-ea8d55c5707d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1061 44297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cfc9c5f1-5579-598f-a9e2-d23b659763f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1061 44801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3947d2bf-1e5f-5028-8a1b-43cfaacc4546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1065 7628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 175921860454